          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">The Goal</h3>
            <p class="case-text">
              I wanted a cluster that behaves like a real one &mdash; two machines
              on different networks, one of them behind CGNAT with no way to
              accept inbound traffic, sharing a control plane and a single
              public entry point. Anything that only works on one laptop teaches
              you nothing about scheduling, networking or failure.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">Build</h3>
            <ul class="case-list">
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Cluster:</span> K3s server on the cloud VM,
                K3s agent on the edge node, joined over the Tailscale mesh so the
                node-to-node traffic is encrypted and neither side needs an open
                port.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Releases:</span> every workload installed
                with Helm rather than loose <code>kubectl apply</code>, so values
                are the only thing that differs between the Hetzner and Azure
                deployments.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Ingress &amp; TLS:</span> ingress-nginx as
                the single entry point, cert-manager issuing and renewing Let's
                Encrypt certificates automatically.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Observability:</span> kube-prometheus-stack
                for Prometheus, Grafana and the node exporters &mdash; the
                dashboards are how I found out what the cluster was actually
                doing rather than what I assumed.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">Autoscaling Under Load</h3>
            <p class="case-text">
              A HorizontalPodAutoscaler drives the deployment off live CPU
              metrics. I load it with ApacheBench and watch the reaction on
              Grafana rather than trusting the manifest.
            </p>
            <!-- VERIFY: every number below must match your own measured run.
                 Screenshot the Grafana dashboard during the run for the README. -->
            <ul class="case-list">
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Load:</span>
                <span class="ph-inline">[N]</span> concurrent clients,
                <span class="ph-inline">[N]</span> total requests via ApacheBench.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Reaction:</span> scaled from 2 to
                <span class="ph-inline">8</span> replicas in
                <span class="ph-inline">[N]</span> seconds, spreading pods across
                both nodes.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Recovery:</span> scaled back down after the
                stabilisation window once the load stopped.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">Chaos Engineering &mdash; The Useful Failure</h3>
            <div class="border border-gray-800 p-5">
              <h4 class="font-serif text-lg mb-2">
                The edge node suspended itself mid-test
              </h4>
              <p class="case-text !mb-3">
                Alongside the deliberate node drains and pod kills, I had one
                genuinely unplanned failure: the physical edge node went to sleep
                while under load. The cluster did exactly the right thing &mdash;
                the node went <code>NotReady</code>, its pods were evicted, and
                the deployment rescheduled them onto the cloud node without a
                request being dropped.
              </p>
              <p class="case-text !mb-3">
                <span class="case-k">The part worth understanding:</span> when
                the edge node woke up and rejoined, the pods did
                <em>not</em> come back to it. The deployment's replica count was
                already satisfied, and Kubernetes has no trigger that
                re-evaluates placement for pods that are already running happily.
                Rebalancing is not a scheduler feature &mdash; it needs something
                external such as a descheduler, or pod topology spread
                constraints applied at admission time.
              </p>
              <p class="case-text !mb-0 text-gray-400">
                That distinction &mdash; between "the scheduler failed" and "the
                scheduler was never asked again" &mdash; is the single most useful
                thing this cluster has taught me.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">CI/CD and Infrastructure as Code</h3>
            <ul class="case-list">
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">GitHub Actions:</span> every push runs the
                unit tests and validates the Kubernetes manifests with
                kubeconform before anything else, then builds the image from a
                multi-stage <code>Dockerfile</code> and pushes it to GHCR on tag.
                A broken manifest or a failing test stops in CI, not in the
                cluster.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Terraform:</span> the VMs, networks and
                firewall rules are declared in HCL against the
                <code>hcloud</code> provider, with an <code>azurerm</code> variant
                for the Azure deployment. Rebuilding the cluster from nothing is
                an <code>apply</code>, not an afternoon.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Portability check:</span> I redeployed the
                identical Helm releases onto Azure VMs to prove nothing had
                quietly grown roots in one provider's assumptions.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <div class="pt-2">
            <a
              href="https://github.com/akos050607/K3d-chaos-cluster"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener"
              class="case-cta">Repository on GitHub -></a
            >
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
