    <!-- ============ Case study modal: Keycloak identity ============ -->
    <div
      id="keycloak-modal"
      class="case-modal fixed inset-0 z-[100] hidden items-center justify-center p-4 md:p-8"
      role="dialog"
      aria-modal="true"
      aria-labelledby="keycloak-modal-title"
    >
      <div data-modal-close class="absolute inset-0 bg-black/80 backdrop-blur-sm">
      </div>

      <div class="case-modal-sheet">
        <div class="case-modal-head">
          <div>
            <div class="mono-label-red !mb-2">[ Case_Study ]</div>
            <h2
              id="keycloak-modal-title"
              class="font-serif text-2xl md:text-3xl leading-tight"
            >
              Putting a Real Identity Provider in Front of My Own Applications
            </h2>
          </div>
          <button
            type="button"
            data-modal-close
            aria-label="Close case study"
            class="case-modal-close">✕</button
          >
        </div>

        <div class="px-6 py-8 md:px-10 md:py-10 space-y-10">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-3 text-center">
            <div class="border border-gray-800 p-3">
              <div class="font-mono text-lg md:text-xl text-nothing-red">OIDC</div>
              <div class="stat-cap">Auth Code + PKCE</div>
            </div>
            <div class="border border-gray-800 p-3">
              <div class="font-mono text-lg md:text-xl text-nothing-red">SAML</div>
              <div class="stat-cap">2.0 Client</div>
            </div>
            <div class="border border-gray-800 p-3">
              <div class="font-mono text-lg md:text-xl text-nothing-red">0</div>
              <div class="stat-cap">Passwords In My App</div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">Why</h3>
            <p class="case-text">
              Every project I had written stored its own users and its own
              password hashes. That is the wrong answer in almost every
              production environment, and it meant I had never actually
              implemented the protocols that enterprises run on. So I stopped
              writing authentication and started delegating it: one Keycloak
              realm on my cluster, and my applications reduced to consumers of
              tokens they did not issue.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">Deployment</h3>
            <ul class="case-list">
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Helm:</span> Keycloak installed as a Helm
                release on the K3s cluster, in its own namespace, with values
                pinned in the repository rather than typed at a prompt.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Persistence:</span> a PostgreSQL instance
                with a PersistentVolumeClaim behind it, so realm configuration,
                clients and users survive a pod restart &mdash; the default
                in-memory database quietly loses everything, which I found out the
                obvious way.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Exposure:</span> published through the
                existing ingress-nginx with a cert-manager certificate. Keycloak
                refuses to issue tokens over plain HTTP for good reason, so TLS
                was a prerequisite rather than a finishing touch.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">The Authorization Code Flow, End to End</h3>
            <p class="case-text">
              SpendSmart &mdash; my existing expense-tracking app &mdash; became
              the protected resource. It no longer knows what a password is.
            </p>
            <ol class="case-steps">
              <li>
                Unauthenticated request hits the app; it redirects the browser to
                the Keycloak realm's authorization endpoint with a client ID,
                requested scopes, a redirect URI and a PKCE challenge.
              </li>
              <li>
                Keycloak authenticates the user itself and redirects back with a
                single-use authorization code.
              </li>
              <li>
                The app exchanges that code, plus the PKCE verifier, for an ID
                token and an access token at the token endpoint &mdash; a
                back-channel call the browser never sees.
              </li>
              <li>
                The app validates the token signature against the realm's JWKS
                endpoint, checks issuer, audience and expiry, and maps the realm
                roles in the token onto its own authorisation rules.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">What Actually Broke</h3>
            <ul class="case-list">
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Redirect URI mismatch:</span> the most common
                failure in the entire protocol, and deliberately unforgiving
                &mdash; the registered URI has to match exactly, because a loose
                match is an open redirect and a token theft primitive.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Proxy headers:</span> behind an ingress,
                Keycloak builds its issuer and redirect URLs from the forwarded
                headers. Until the proxy mode was configured correctly it
                generated internal-looking URLs that the browser could not reach.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Clock skew:</span> token validation compares
                <code>exp</code> and <code>iat</code> against the verifying
                machine's clock. A drifting node produces tokens that are somehow
                already expired, or not yet valid.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Header buffer sizes:</span> tokens carrying a
                lot of role claims outgrew the ingress's default header buffer,
                which surfaces as an opaque 502 rather than anything mentioning
                tokens.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">OIDC and SAML, Side by Side</h3>
            <p class="case-text">
              I registered a second client using SAML 2.0 against the same realm
              specifically so I would have a first-hand opinion rather than a
              memorised one.
            </p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
              <div class="border border-gray-800 p-4">
                <div class="mono-label-red !mb-3 !text-[10px]">[ OIDC ]</div>
                <p class="case-text !text-xs !mb-0">
                  JSON and JWTs over a REST-shaped flow. Light enough for SPAs and
                  mobile clients, PKCE covers public clients that cannot keep a
                  secret. The default choice for anything new.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="border border-gray-800 p-4">
                <div class="mono-label-red !mb-3 !text-[10px]">[ SAML 2.0 ]</div>
                <p class="case-text !text-xs !mb-0">
                  Signed XML assertions over browser POST bindings. Heavier, but
                  it is what a large amount of existing enterprise software
                  actually speaks &mdash; which is why "legacy" is a description,
                  not a reason to avoid learning it.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">What This Changed</h3>
            <p class="case-text">
              The interesting part was never the login screen. It was
              understanding that identity is infrastructure: a component you
              deploy, secure, persist, expose over TLS and monitor like any other
              &mdash; and that once it exists, every other service you run gets to
              stop inventing its own answer to the same question.
            </p>
          </section>

